    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_226d3955d1fc1eadf2a384816366bfb540161389df87e7fab8bbf527dd9beab8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Repeated Poisson or van der Corput transformation contracts an exponential sum only when an intervening normalization prevents inversion back to the original representation and the transformed phase remains in a reusable family. Quadratic phases have exact Gaussian closure, whereas cubic phases recover only approximate closure by using coefficient smallness to retain the quadratic and cubic terms and expand higher terms and amplitude corrections into controlled weights and error.",
