    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_4c901b24df908fa96f332d6ca8e1fad5131c134f6a67575f18c0e524e170b3bd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Saddle reduction produces bounded-degree polynomial-phase sums whose degree rises when the terminal length is small relative to the inherited scale. Independent-coordinate FFT gridding is affordable while that degree is uniformly bounded, but in the high-degree short-length regime the higher coefficients must instead be kept in their shared analytic parameterization: expressing them through τ, ρ, and γ turns a growing coefficient vector into a six-variable interpolation problem and restores the preprocessing budget.",
