    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_6f69cdf222357c7f9ce6eca3ff5fc219aa5669b6e492237f23b16ce96c3c9a5d",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Choosing longer Riemann–Siegel blocks reduces the number of outer blocks but turns each block into a polynomial-phase query of higher degree or wider coefficient range. The complete reduction is useful only when truncation error, direct remainders, per-query work, and reusable preprocessing all fit the same global exponent; in the cubic case, recursive shortening increases the cubic coefficient until a boundary solver or a final precomputed transform must absorb the resulting representation complexity.",
